From: aiueo52 <aiueo52@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 15:09:19 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 087/105] C1: exact downward prediction, hazard upward, and
 anti-oscillation

Replaces the estimator. The two directions are not symmetric and pretending
they are was leaving accuracy on the table.

DOWNWARD is exact with no model. A topk=1 draft is a greedy chain, the target
accepts a prefix, and the first S' tokens of a length-S chain are the tokens a
length-S' chain would have drafted -- so a step observed at S says exactly what
S' < S would have accepted: min(accepted, S'). Validated on the 2026-09-06
traces, mean(min(a_15,3)) from the W16 run against the separately measured W4
run: 2.66/2.76, 1.49/1.47, 1.14/1.35, 2.09/2.10.

UPWARD is censored -- at S=3 an accepted count of 3 could mean 3 or 14 -- and
that is where the confidence earns its keep. What survives censoring is the
boundary hazard r = P(a>=S)/P(a>=S-1); modelling per-position acceptance as flat after
position 2, that one rate extends the chain. Predicting the W16 runs from
the W4 traces: +14%, -8%, +17%, +9%. Optimistic, hence tail_bias=0.9. The
upward estimate is always recomputed from the live state, never read from a
remembered EMA of the other state, which would go stale exactly when the
workload changes.

Also adds grace backoff: each REVERSAL of the previous switch multiplies the
grace window. Without it the controller ping-pongs where the two candidates
genuinely tie (offline on agent-loop: 24 switches over 3000 batches). A tie
costs almost nothing to hold, precisely because it is a tie.

Recommended config saved as adaptive/w16_conf.json (margin 0.20, rate_alpha
0.05, grace 40, interval 20, backoff 3.0). Offline on the recorded traces,
3000-batch replay, against the better fixed profile per workload:
  shipped EMA   worst 0.957  mean 0.986   43 switches
  confidence    worst 0.965  mean 0.985    5 switches

